From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gustavo Noronha Silva <gustavo@noronha.dev.br>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 13:22:27 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] venus: allocate dma-buf import memory synchronously
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

vn_device_memory_import_dma_buf went through
vn_device_memory_alloc_simple, which submits vkAllocateMemory
asynchronously and returns VK_SUCCESS as soon as the ring accepts the
command. For plain allocations that is a good trade: failure means the
host is out of memory and everything is about to go wrong anyway. For
imports it is not: the host can reject the import for expected runtime
reasons (the underlying virtio-gpu resource may not be attachable in the
renderer), and the async success leaves the application holding a ghost
VkDeviceMemory the host never created. The next command naming that
handle fails the renderer-side object lookup and poisons the ring — one
failed import kills the whole context, and every subsequent command in
it, instead of surfacing VK_ERROR_INVALID_EXTERNAL_HANDLE to the one
vkAllocateMemory that could have handled it.

Allocate synchronously on the import path, the same trade
VN_PERF(NO_ASYNC_MEM_ALLOC) makes globally, applied unconditionally to
the one path where failure is an expected runtime state. Imports are
not on the hot allocation path, and the caller already does a ring
roundtrip just before this call.

Signed-off-by: Gustavo Noronha Silva <gustavo@noronha.dev.br>
---
 src/virtio/vulkan/vn_device_memory.c | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_device_memory.c b/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_device_memory.c
index 213830aa9be..033d555f16a 100644
--- a/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_device_memory.c
+++ b/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_device_memory.c
@@ -136,7 +136,19 @@ vn_device_memory_import_dma_buf(struct vn_device *dev,
       .allocationSize = alloc_info->allocationSize,
       .memoryTypeIndex = alloc_info->memoryTypeIndex,
    };
-   result = vn_device_memory_alloc_simple(dev, mem, &memory_allocate_info);
+   /* The import allocation must be synchronous. A dma-buf import can fail
+    * host-side for expected runtime reasons (the resource may not be
+    * attachable in the renderer — unlike a plain allocation, where failure
+    * means OOM), and the async path reports VK_SUCCESS on ring-accept: a
+    * host-side rejection then leaves the app holding a ghost VkDeviceMemory,
+    * and the next command naming it poisons the ring — one failed import
+    * kills the whole context. Same trade VN_PERF(NO_ASYNC_MEM_ALLOC) makes,
+    * applied unconditionally to the one path where failure is expected.
+    */
+   VkDevice dev_handle = vn_device_to_handle(dev);
+   VkDeviceMemory mem_handle = vn_device_memory_to_handle(mem);
+   result = vn_call_vkAllocateMemory(dev->primary_ring, dev_handle,
+                                     &memory_allocate_info, NULL, &mem_handle);
    if (result != VK_SUCCESS) {
       vn_renderer_bo_unref(dev->renderer, bo);
       return result;
